{
  "id": "80520",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Preparation of the Transition Elements",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Ancient civilizations knew about iron, copper, silver, and gold. The time periods in human history known as the Bronze Age and the Iron Age mark the advancements in which societies learned to isolate certain metals and use them to make tools and goods. Naturally occurring ores of copper, silver, and gold can contain high concentrations of these metals in elemental form (see figure below). Iron, on the other hand, occurs on earth almost exclusively in oxidized forms, such as rust (Fe"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O). The earliest known iron implements were made from iron meteorites. Surviving iron artifacts dating from approximately 4000 to 2500 BC are rare, but all known examples contain specific alloys of iron and nickel that occur only in extraterrestrial objects, not on earth. It took thousands of years of technological advances before civilizations developed iron smelting, the ability to extract a pure element from its naturally occurring ores, and for iron tools to become common."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2134900571",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Transition metals occur in nature in various forms. Examples include "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " a nugget of copper, "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " a deposit of gold, and "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(c)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " an ore containing oxidized iron. (Credit a: Modification of work by http://images-of-elements.com/copper-2.jpg. Credit c: Modification of work by http://images-of-elements.com/iron-ore.jpg.)"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1735451205",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "cpe0r6itzitwpnb",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 227,
            "id": "3292923074",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/EC/ECDAEB35E80CF38569B08EAA192D1926/trans_metals_nature.jpeg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 675
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "832640902",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "1595051872",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Usually, the transition elements are extracted from minerals found in a variety of ores. The ease of their recovery varies widely, depending on the concentration of the element in the ore, the identity of the other elements present, and the difficulty of reducing the element to the free metal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1218053384",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In general, it is not difficult to reduce ions of the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block elements to the free element. Carbon is a sufficiently strong reducing agent in most cases. However, like the ions of the more active main group metals, ions of the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block elements must be isolated by "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "electrolysis"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A process using electrical energy to cause a nonspontaneous process to occur."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4099392268",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3110558065",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " or by "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "reduction"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A process in which a reactant gains one or more electrons resulting in a decrease in oxidation number."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "585739098",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1743064702",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " with an active metal such as calcium."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1383633699",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "There are many interesting and unique processes for isolating metals, and each is specific for the element involved. We discuss the processes used for the isolation of iron and copper because these processes illustrate the principal means of isolating "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block metals. In general, each process involves three principal steps: preliminary treatment, smelting, and refining."
              }
            ],
            "id": "149148721",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "Preliminary treatment."
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " Initial treatment of the ores to make them suitable for the extraction of the metals usually involves crushing or grinding the ore, concentrating the metal-bearing components, and sometimes treating these substances chemically to convert them into compounds that are easier to reduce to the metal."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3367097515",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1212070241",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "Smelting."
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " Extraction of the metal in the molten state, a process called "
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "smelting"
                          }
                        ],
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "children": [
                              {
                                "text": "The process of extracting a pure metal from a molten ore."
                              }
                            ],
                            "id": "2803422522",
                            "type": "p"
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "2530352335",
                        "trigger": "hover",
                        "type": "popup"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ", includes reduction of the metallic compound to the metal. Impurities may be removed by adding a compound that forms a slag—a substance with a low melting point that can be readily separated from the molten metal."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1816825821",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3979119585",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "strong": true,
                        "text": "Refining."
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " The final step in the recovery of a metal is refining the metal. Low-boiling metals such as zinc and mercury can be refined by distillation. When fused on an inclined table, low-melting metals such as tin flow away from higher-melting impurities. Electrolysis is another common method for refining metals."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2998297341",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2663642882",
                "type": "li"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2988273104",
            "type": "ol"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "cyggr83k4f68l2c",
                "text": "Isolation of Iron"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3664742805",
            "type": "h2"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The early application of iron to the manufacture of tools and weapons was possible because of the wide distribution of iron ores and the ease with which iron compounds in the ores could be reduced by carbon. For a long time, charcoal was the form of carbon used in the reduction process. The production and use of iron became much more widespread when coke was introduced as the reducing agent around 1620. Coke is a form of carbon formed by heating coal in the absence of air to remove impurities."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1576594899",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The first step in the metallurgy of iron is usually to roast the ore (heat the ore in air to remove water), decompose carbonates into oxides, and convert sulfides into oxides. The oxides are then reduced in a blast furnace that is 80 to 100 feet high and about 25 feet in diameter (see image below) in which the roasted ore, coke, and limestone (impure CaCO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ") are introduced continuously into the top. Molten iron and slag are withdrawn at the bottom. The entire stock in a furnace may weigh several hundred tons."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3866475192",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Within a blast furnace, different reactions occur in different temperature zones. Carbon monoxide is generated in the hotter bottom regions and rises upward to reduce the iron oxides to pure iron through a series of reactions that take place in the upper regions."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1694321529",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "k4omyhpe3hdloal",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 414,
            "id": "2645653475",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/14/14BA54757BB6DF76CE501C610D4C4D30/blast_furnace_1.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 567.9322916666666
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3543347295",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "2254669375",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Near the bottom of a furnace are nozzles through which preheated air is blown into the furnace. As soon as the air enters, the coke in the region of the nozzles is oxidized to carbon dioxide with the liberation of a great deal of heat. The hot carbon dioxide passes upward through the overlying layer of white-hot coke, where it is reduced to carbon monoxide:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4045936394",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "43rqk9plax1e8y5",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1153979694",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{CO}}_2(g)\\;+\\;\\mathrm C(s)\\;\\rightarrow\\;2\\mathrm{CO}(g)\n",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The carbon monoxide serves as the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "reducing agent"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A substance that brings about the reduction of another substance, and in the process becomes oxidized. Also called "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "reductant"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "156971420",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2376440960",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " in the upper regions of the furnace. The individual reactions are indicated in the figure above."
              }
            ],
            "id": "980715904",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The iron oxides are reduced in the upper region of the furnace. In the middle region, limestone (calcium carbonate) decomposes, and the resulting calcium oxide combines with silica and silicates in the ore to form slag. The slag is mostly calcium silicate (CaSiO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and contains most of the commercially unimportant components of the ore:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4139846984",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "06io9h45vlzqujv",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1565627182",
            "src": "\\mathrm{CaO}(s)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm{SiO}}_2(s)\\;\\longrightarrow\\;{\\mathrm{CaSiO}}_3(l)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Just below the middle of the furnace, the temperature is high enough to melt both the iron and the slag. They collect in layers at the bottom of the furnace; the less dense slag floats on the iron and protects it from oxidation. Several times a day, the slag and molten iron are withdrawn from the furnace. The iron is transferred to casting machines or to a steelmaking plant (see image below)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "657025181",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Molten iron is shown being cast as steel. (Credit: Clint Budd.)"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "309552541",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ogfa4kfkgh134aa",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 434.125,
            "id": "1562966211",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/05/05F9F14749E392FAF3C616F9140DE281/molten_iron.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 435.4773613369644
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3919874808",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "4110544819",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Much of the iron produced is refined and converted into steel. Steel is made from iron by removing impurities and adding substances such as manganese, chromium, nickel, tungsten, molybdenum, and vanadium to produce alloys with properties that make the material suitable for specific uses. Most steels also contain small but definite percentages of carbon (0.04%–2.5%). However, a large part of the carbon contained in iron must be removed in the manufacture of steel; otherwise, the excess carbon would make the iron brittle."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1441465758",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "300245812",
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "activity_id": 79881,
            "children": [],
            "id": "2938823359",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1826886308",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
            "id": "1079694609",
            "textDirection": "ltr",
            "type": "content"
          }
        ],
        "id": "930616235",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "learnbydoing",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "activity_id": 80455,
            "children": [],
            "id": "2940698948",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          }
        ],
        "id": "220204846",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "learnbydoing",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "if0rdn0p0eag1be",
                "text": "Isolation of Copper"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2074299099",
            "type": "h2"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The most important ores of copper contain copper sulfides (such as covellite, CuS), although copper oxides (such as tenorite, CuO) and copper hydroxycarbonates (such as malachite, Cu"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ") are sometimes found. In the production of copper metal, the concentrated sulfide ore is roasted to remove part of the sulfur as sulfur dioxide. The remaining mixture, which consists of Cu"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "S, FeS, FeO, and SiO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is mixed with limestone, which serves as a flux (a material that aids in the removal of impurities), and heated. Molten slag forms as the iron and silica are removed by Lewis acid–base reactions:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2508087844",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "v76xxf2h2f1g4g1",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3247600946",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{CaCO}}_3(s)+{\\mathrm{SiO}}_2(s)\\longrightarrow{\\mathrm{CaSiO}}_3(l)+{\\mathrm{CO}}_2(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "rjqlhu726nqp4nd",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "1278306023",
            "src": "\\mathrm{FeO}(s)+{\\mathrm{SiO}}_2(s)\\longrightarrow{\\mathrm{FeSiO}}_3(l)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In these reactions, the silicon dioxide behaves as a Lewis acid, which accepts a pair of electrons from the Lewis base (the oxide ion)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "91014629",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Reduction of the Cu"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "S that remains after smelting is accomplished by blowing air through the molten material. The air converts part of the Cu"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "S into Cu"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O. As soon as copper(I) oxide is formed, it is reduced by the remaining copper(I) sulfide to metallic copper:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "604658510",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "9sszl2pv41bx2kz",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3876700181",
            "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm{Cu}}_2\\mathrm S(l)+3\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\;\\longrightarrow\\;2\\;{\\mathrm{Cu}}_2\\mathrm O(l)+2\\;{\\mathrm{SO}}_2(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "r93xs4477ky6bki",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3660865421",
            "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm{Cu}}_2\\mathrm O(l)+{\\mathrm{Cu}}_2\\mathrm S(l)\\;\\longrightarrow\\;6\\;\\mathrm{Cu}(l)+{\\mathrm{SO}}_2(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The copper obtained in this way is called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "blister copper"
              },
              {
                "text": " because of its characteristic appearance, which is due to the air blisters it contains (see figure below). This impure copper is cast into large plates, which are used as anodes in the electrolytic refining of the metal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1611320747",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "nreqfqv7he08twr",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "3456965191",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/DB/DB0DAA4C4F1742655452048472FA1EDA/blister_copper.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 300
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Blister copper is obtained during the conversion of copper-containing ore into pure copper. (Credit: “Tortie tude”/Wikimedia Commons.)"
              }
            ],
            "id": "837526480",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
